# Files that must reach the working tree byte-for-byte as they were committed.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf` is `true` by default in Git for Windows: it strips CR on commit and
# re-adds it on checkout. That is right for source, and wrong for anything a build step compares
# byte-for-byte -- the file on disk is then never the file that was committed.
#
# `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt` is generated by `scripts/notices.ps1` and diffed in CI, so this was
# not a theoretical concern: the check could not pass on any platform.
#
#   - The generated file carries 65 literal CR bytes, inside the licence texts of crates whose
#     own LICENSE files are CRLF. Git normalised those away on commit, so the committed file was
#     65 bytes shorter than anything `cargo about` produces.
#   - On a Windows runner, checkout then re-expanded *every* LF, making the file ~9000 bytes
#     longer instead. The two failures looked unrelated and neither named line endings.
#   - `about.hbs` is here for the same reason from the other direction: converted to CRLF on
#     checkout, the template emits CRLF, so the generated file differs by ~1000 bytes before
#     anything is even compared.
#
# The symptom is a diff with mismatched byte counts and no differing *lines*, which is the
# signature of an EOL problem and worth recognising.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt -text
about.hbs -text
